	"publication_text": "The figure in the back of this miniature two-person kayak (iqyax in Unangam Tunuu) wears the type of seal-gut garment worn by hunters to keep dry and protected from rain and ocean waters. The brim on the conical wooden hat would shield the hunter’s eyes from glare, while the seal whiskers extending from the hat’s back would announce the hunter’s prowess and number of kills. Such historic models can be valuable sources of information for contemporary craftspeople dedicated to learning and reviving ancestral forms of technology. (Olcott, Hillary C. et. al., Yua, Spirit of the Arctic: Highlights from the Thomas G. Fowler Collection (San Francisco: Fine Arts Museums of San Francisco, 2020), pp. 4-5, 144, no. 68, entry by Roslyn Tunis., consulting curator, Thomas G. Fowler Collection.)",
